# KTP live field overlay

Sprint doc: `docs/sprints/revision-patient-ktp-live-field-overlay-ocr-1.md`.
Extends rules 177 (OCR is a suggestion), 178 (who may use it), 179 (how a value
is read) and 180 (consent first). None of them is relaxed; where this rule is
silent, they govern.

## What it is

While the camera is open, `resources/js/ktp-live-camera.js` (lazy chunk) reads a
DOWNSCALED copy of the preview a few times a second, finds the card with the
shipped detector, and draws the card outline and the template's field boxes over
the video as an SVG layer, with a RED / YELLOW / GREEN indicator and plain
instructions. The geometry, quality and capture rules are pure functions in
`resources/js/ktp-live-overlay.js` (node-tested); the analysis runs in
`resources/js/ktp-live-tracker.worker.js` when the browser has module workers,
otherwise on the main thread. OCR still runs once, after the photo is taken.

## Foundations

1. **D7 consent before ANY camera processing.** The camera opens only after the
   KTP holder's "yes" (rule 180 §3), and the live module is imported only after
   the camera opened. Every analysis tick re-asks `liveTrackingAllowed()` (OCR on
   the page + accepted consent + usable wording, defined once in
   `ktp-camera-ocr.js`); a "no", a closed camera or a cleared page ends tracking
   before the next frame is read. Clearing the photo resets consent AND closes
   the camera opened under it, and `capture()` refuses once tracking ended.
2. **Live tracking never runs before consent** — not on a static illustration,
   not "in the background", not to warm anything up.
3. **One coordinate model.** Field boxes are the versioned template
   (`KTP_TEMPLATE` via `templateBoxes`), normalized 0..1 on the card; the live
   overlay maps them through the card homography. Never keep a second ROI
   coordinate system, and never hard-code pixel boxes for the preview.
4. **The overlay follows verified geometry only.** A detected quad is validated
   before a homography is built from it (`assessGeometry`): four finite points,
   convex and in `[tl, tr, br, bl]` order as given (a bow-tie is refused, never
   repaired), inside the frame bounds, a well-defined homography over the whole
   card, card-shaped (a portrait-held card is its own reason), perspective under
   the rejection bound.
5. **Invalid geometry fails safe.** No card means RED with a neutral hint frame —
   never a guessed outline. The mapping onto the screen is computed from the
   video's layout box in CSS pixels (`clientWidth/Height`), honouring
   `object-fit` and a mirroring CSS transform; device pixel ratio and page zoom
   do not move it.
6. **The overlay is never part of the photo.** It is a separate SVG layer; the
   capture draws only the video frame into its canvases. Do not draw, serialize
   or composite the overlay into an image.
7. **OCR runs after capture, never on every frame.** The preview analysis is
   bounded: never two at once, at most every `minIntervalMs`, slower when the
   device is slower (`cpuBudget`), and a worker that stops answering is dropped
   for the main-thread path.
8. **Capture geometry comes from the captured pixels.** The live corners, frozen
   when the button is pressed, are only COMPARED with a detection on the captured
   frame (`confirmCaptureGeometry`). A capture where the card is not found passes
   NO corners on (`unconfirmed`): the read detects the edge itself or the
   operator sets the corners. Stale live geometry is never used.
9. **The existing ROI OCR is reused** (rule 179) — the field read takes the
   capture-confirmed corners as `boundary.reason = 'live'`, distinct from an
   operator corner edit (`manual`). Boxes stay editable, BACA ULANG stays per
   field, the corner editor stays available.
10. **Whole-document OCR remains** the companion read and the fallback
    (rule 179 §1); a field pipeline failure still falls back to it.
11. **The photo keeps the card's original pixels.** Only a `confirmed` capture
    (the live card and the card found on the captured pixels agree) is cropped,
    to the card's bounding box plus a margin; any other outcome keeps the whole
    frame, so one wrong detection never cuts the stored document. Nothing is
    warped. A lossless copy is read by OCR; the uploaded document is the JPEG it
    always was. A pending lossless copy and corner hint belong to one photo:
    a new photo, a manual file or a scan discards them, and the confirm handler
    takes the photo (upload, lossless copy, hint) as ONE object before its first
    `await` — while an upload and read are in flight, no other photo, camera or
    scan may start.
12. **Operator verification stays mandatory.** GREEN means geometry and image
    look suitable, never that the read is right (rule 177 §4). The screen says so.
13. **OCR results stay suggestions** and are applied only by explicit selection
    ("Terapkan"), never over a value silently; registration is never submitted
    automatically.
14. **NIK masking, private KTP storage and branch isolation** are unchanged. The
    overlay draws only the template's own field labels — never OCR text — and
    its statistics are counts and timings only (no corners, pixels or text).
15. **The pilot gate stays the single decider** (rule 178). The overlay markup is
    inside the camera block, which only a pilot-eligible operator receives. A
    release never arms the flag, widens the cohort or extends the period.
16. **Scanner and manual upload remain** complete, consent-free paths for
    registration without OCR (rule 180 §6).
17. **No automatic clinical rollout.** Engineering GO is not a real-device,
    real-card or clinical result. Fake-camera and synthetic-frame runs are
    browser-only evidence; real-device and real-KTP results are measured on site
    under consent, or reported `PENDING`.
18. **No network, no storage.** The live code makes no request, uses no
    browser storage and sends nothing to any third party; the worker returns
    numbers only.
19. **Thresholds are calibrated, not invented.** The quality limits in
    `LIVE_LIMITS` were set from the synthetic frames of
    `tools/ktp-ocr-benchmark/generate_live_frames.py`; change them only from
    measured pilot data, and record the measurement.
20. **Tailwind only compiles classes it finds in Blade.** Indicator variants are
    pre-rendered in the view; SVG colours are design-token hex values set as
    attributes in JS. Never add a Tailwind class only in JS.

## Release discipline

- Production deployment runs `scripts/deploy-vps-runner.sh` ON the VPS, never
  locally; a started deploy is not a finished one (`exit=0` + `DEPLOY OK`).
- A GO tag needs the evidence first and is never moved.
- Cleanup removes only task-owned processes, worktrees and files.

## Gotchas

- `npm run build` emits the worker as a self-contained IIFE constructed with
  `{ type: 'module' }`; an IIFE is valid module code. A browser without module
  workers throws on construction, which the overlay turns into the main-thread
  path. Do not "fix" a browser test by removing `window.Worker` altogether —
  tesseract.js needs a classic worker and OCR would fail for a reason no real
  browser has.
- With a live hint the pilot metric `card_found` means "found by the
  capture-time detection on the photo" — still a detection on the stored pixels,
  never assumed from the preview.
- Measuring burn-in by colour must use a colour the card does not contain: the
  fictional card has red elements, so the GREEN stroke colour is the probe, and
  the measure is paired with a control that draws the outline in and must see it.
